{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Prior distribution for animal mass is positive semi-axis (θ > 0) with upper bound θ_max = 30 tons (blue whale mass)",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:47:40\u201300:48:13] 'First, they start from prior distribution of possible masses of animals on our planet. So this distribution has restricted support. So of course we understand that mass cannot be negative. So it's a positive semi-axis. And it has some form where, again, there's a theta max because no animal may have mass more than 30 tons. That is the mass of the blue whale.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "First wisdomer uses probabilistic model P(x | θ) where x is tactile observation and θ is animal mass",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:48:44\u201300:49:16] 'this is kind of why with the function. So it shows that if an animal has a mass of theta, how probable is that I feel, what I feel, that I will measure x.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "First wisdomer updates prior to posterior distribution P(θ | x) ∝ P(x | θ)P(θ)",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:49:16\u201300:49:51] 'he performs Bayesian inference, and he comes up with a posterior distribution. So this is his model, or his likelihood. X is something he observes, times prior distribution, or a normalization constant.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Example: Touching elephant's nose leads to posterior suggesting either small animal or very large animal (e.g., 'b model four')",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:49:51\u201300:50:27] 'So this is either nose of some very large animal, or this is a body of some snake-like animal. And so by doing Bayesian inference, he updates the distribution on theta, and it has now, say, b model four.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Second wisdomer uses probabilistic model P(y | θ) where y is acoustic observation",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:50:56\u201300:51:26] 'So he has completely different probabilistic model. So Q, Y, given theta. So Y is completely different variable. It lives in completely different dimension.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Second wisdomer updates posterior using posterior from first wisdom as new prior: P(θ | x, y) ∝ P(y | θ)P(θ | x)",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:51:26\u201300:52:06] 'he uses posterior distribution as a new prior distribution. He multiplies it by its own likelihood. He normalizes the result.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Sequential Bayesian updating leads to posterior distribution that becomes sharply concentrated around true mass as observations accumulate",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:52:43\u201300:53:21] 'we will probably end up with a posterior distribution that will be pretty sharp around some small area.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Bayesian framework allows combining multiple probabilistic models with different observation spaces into a single posterior distribution",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:52:43\u201300:53:21] 'We can combine different probabilistic models and we can build a more complicated model from a simple ones like a wall from building blocks.'"
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "Note: 'b model four' and 'Maxwell's estimator' are likely misstatements in the ASR (probably 'posterior distribution' and 'maximum likelihood estimator')."
}
